Exactly. Imagination is powerful, but intention becomes consequential when it turns into action.

An idea becomes a story. A story changes what people believe. Belief changes behavior. Behavior changes incentives, institutions, relationships, and eventually the physical world.

And sometimes the consequences are nothing like what the creator imagined.

That’s why there’s a difference between what you can imagine, what you can create, what you should create, and what you can responsibly sustain.

There’s also a subtle danger in framing: you can become so successful at making others see the world through your lens that you forget it’s a lens rather than the whole of reality.

Which brings us back to the Pantheon.

The ability to bring possibilities into existence needs a corresponding ability to recognize when not to.

Not everything possible needs to become actual. Not every powerful idea needs to be unleashed.

And perhaps the highest expression of creative power isn’t simply making something happen.

It’s understanding what happens because you did.